course: dictionary
lesson: 2131
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «You smell cookies baking from the other room.» Here a cookie is something you can eat, because it is baking and you can smell it.
explain: «A cookie is a small file a website saves in your browser to remember things like your login or basket.» This sentence is about a computer, not food. The words browser, login and website are the clues. Always ask: which meaning fits the other words around it?
explain: Places can change words too. «A British "biscuit" is an American "cookie".» People in the USA and Canada say cookie, while people in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand say biscuit. In maths problems, a cookie is simply a counted thing, so «shared between 7» tells you to divide.
fact: A cookie can mean a small sweet baked biscuit, often round, sometimes with chocolate chips.
fact: On a computer, a cookie is a small file a website keeps about your visit.
fact: «Accept cookies» on a website means you allow the site to save these small files.
fact: In the USA and Canada, «cookie» is the usual word; in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, the same treat is called a «biscuit».
fact: Check the other words in the sentence: they tell you which meaning of a word is the right one.
exercise: An American says «cookie». What would a British child say for the same treat? | *Biscuit | Bread roll | Scone | Pavement
exercise: «A cookie is a small file a website saves in your browser to remember your login.» What is a cookie in this sentence? | Bread roll | Small sweet baked treat | *Small file on a computer | Kind of lift
exercise: A maths story says, «Ana shared 12 cookies between 4 friends.» Which operation does shared between tell you to use? | Subtract | Multiply | *Divide | Add
test: Which word is the American word for a British biscuit? | Bun | *Cookie | Scone | Roll
test: «Accept cookies» on a website means what? | Cleaning your browser | Ordering food | *Letting the site save small files about your visit | Baking a treat
exam: «Some cookies track you across sites, so sites must ask first.» What does this tell you about cookies on a computer? | They are the same as biscuits | Websites never need to ask | Cookies are always sweet | *Websites must ask before some cookies track you
